  "content": {
    "id": "p23-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "The depth $D$ of water in metres at St Kilda pier $t$ hours after high tide can be modelled by the function:\n$$D(t)=a+b\\cos(nt).$$\nAt high tide the water depth is 8 meters. The next low tide occurs 6.5 hours later, with a depth of 2 meters.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find the values of $a$ and $b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$a=5,\\ b=3$.",
          "worked": "The mean depth is $a$ and the amplitude is $b$. The depth is greatest at $t=0$, so $b$ is positive.\n$$\\begin{align*}a&=\\frac{8+2}{2}\\\\a&=5\\\\b&=\\frac{8-2}{2}\\\\b&=3\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find the period of the function. Hence, calculate $n$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Period: 13 hours; $n=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{13}$.",
          "worked": "High tide to the next low tide is half a period.\n$$\\begin{align*}T&=2(6.5)\\\\T&=13\\text{ hours}\\\\n&=\\frac{2\\pi}{T}\\\\n&=\\frac{2\\pi}{13}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of $D(t)$ over a 26-hour interval.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$D(t)=5+3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi t}{13}\\right)$ for $0\\le t\\le26$; see graph.",
          "worked": "$$D(t)=5+3\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{13}\\right),\\qquad 0\\le t\\le26.$$\nThe midline is $D=5$. Maxima occur at $(0,8)$, $(13,8)$ and $(26,8)$; minima occur at $(6.5,2)$ and $(19.5,2)$. Draw a smooth cosine curve through these points.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p23-q3-c-solution-graph",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[{\"name\":\"curveBlue\",\"hex\":\"268CFF\",\"reason\":\"function curve distinguished from axes and guides\"}],\"reference\":\"source page 23 question 3(c); teacher page 24\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.22cm,y=0.36cm]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,0)--(28,0) node[right] {$t$};\n\\draw[->,line width=0.5pt] (0,0)--(0,9.4) node[above] {$D(t)$};\n\\foreach \\y in {2,5,8}{\\draw[dashed,line width=0.4pt] (0,\\y)--(26,\\y);\\node[left] at (0,\\y) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$\\y$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};}\n\\foreach \\x in {0,6.5,13,19.5,26}{\\draw[line width=0.4pt] (\\x,0.15)--(\\x,-0.15);\\node[below] at (\\x,-0.15) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$\\x$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};}\n\\draw[curveBlue,line width=0.8pt,domain=0:26,samples=161] plot (\\x,{5+3*cos(360*\\x/13)});\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 75,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
              "spec": {
                "sourcePage": 23,
                "sourceRegion": "Question 3(c); teacher PDF page 24, printed page 353",
                "description": "Two complete tidal cycles with labelled extrema times and dashed depth guides.",
                "mathematics": "D(t)=5+3cos(2*pi*t/13), 0<=t<=26; maximum 8, minimum 2, period 13.",
                "answerVisibility": "Solution only."
              },
              "derived": false,
              "reviewStatus": "needs-review"
            }
          ]
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "During this interval, for what values of $t$ is the water depth 3.5 meters?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$t=\\dfrac{13}{3},\\ \\dfrac{26}{3},\\ \\dfrac{52}{3},\\ \\dfrac{65}{3}$ hours.\n\nSolve $\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi t}{13}\\right)=-\\dfrac12$ over two periods.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}5+3\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{13}\\right)&=3.5\\\\\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{13}\\right)&=-\\frac12\\end{align*}$$\nFor $0\\le t\\le26$, the angle satisfies $0\\le\\dfrac{2\\pi t}{13}\\le4\\pi$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2\\pi t}{13}&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{4\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{8\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{10\\pi}{3}\\\\t&=\\frac{13}{3},\\ \\frac{26}{3},\\ \\frac{52}{3},\\ \\frac{65}{3}\\text{ hours}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "A pole supporting the pier stands 9 meters above the seabed. What percentage of the pole is exposed at 9 am? Round your answer to the nearest whole number. If this cannot be determined from the given information, explain what additional information is needed.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Cannot be determined without the clock time of high tide.",
          "worked": "Let $t_9$ be the number of hours after high tide at 9 am. The exposed length is $9-D(t_9)$, so\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Percentage exposed}&=\\frac{9-D(t_9)}{9}\\times100\\%\\\\\\text{Percentage exposed}&=\\frac{4-3\\cos(2\\pi t_9/13)}{9}\\times100\\%.\\end{align*}$$\nThe clock time of high tide is not supplied, so $t_9$ and the requested percentage cannot be determined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q3-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "Over time, mussels have attached themselves to the pole. A mussel can survive as long as it isn’t exposed to air for more than 4 hours at a time. Determine the maximum height at which a mussel can survive up the pole. Answer in metres, correct to two decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "3.30 m above the seabed.\n\nThe limiting height equals the water depth two hours either side of low tide.",
          "worked": "The limiting exposure interval lasts 4 hours and is symmetric about low tide at $t=6.5$. Its endpoints are $t=4.5$ and $t=8.5$. At the maximum surviving height $h$, the water reaches the mussel at both endpoints.\n$$\\begin{align*}h&=D(4.5)\\\\h&=5+3\\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi(4.5)}{13}\\right)\\\\h&=5+3\\cos\\left(\\frac{9\\pi}{13}\\right)\\\\h&\\approx3.30\\text{ m}\\end{align*}$$\nA higher mussel would be exposed continuously for more than 4 hours.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
